In Java — which is commonly used for Selenium and other test automation frameworks — method overloading and method overriding are two fundamental Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concepts under Polymorphism. They allow flexibility, code reusability, and better maintainability in automation frameworks.

Method Overloading (Compile-Time Polymorphism)

Definition:

Method Overloading occurs when multiple methods in the same class have the same method name but different parameters (either in number, type, or order). It is also known as compile-time polymorphism, because the method to be executed is decided at compile time.

Key Points:

• Occurs within the same class.
• Methods have same name but different parameter lists.
• The return type may or may not be different.
• It improves code readability and reusability.
• It helps in handling similar actions with different inputs.

Real-Time Example in Test Automation:

Imagine you’re building a Selenium automation framework for testing a web application. You might have a “LoginPage” class where you need to test different types of login — admin login, user login, and login with additional security token.

public class LoginPage {

    // Method 1 - Login with username and password
    public void login(String username, String password) {
        System.out.println("Logging in with username and password.");
        // WebDriver code to enter username, password, and click login
    }

    // Method 2 - Overloaded method: Login with username, password, and OTP
    public void login(String username, String password, String otp) {
        System.out.println("Logging in with username, password, and OTP.");
        // WebDriver code to handle OTP-based login
    }

    // Method 3 - Overloaded method: Login using a credentials object
    public void login(Credentials creds) {
        System.out.println("Logging in using credentials object.");
        // WebDriver code using creds.getUsername() and creds.getPassword()
    }
}

How it works:

• The same method name login() is reused.
• Depending on how many or what kind of arguments you pass, Java determines which version to call at compile time.

Example in Test Script:

LoginPage page = new LoginPage();
// Different login scenarios
page.login("admin", "admin123");
page.login("user", "user123", "456789");
page.login(new Credentials("tester", "test123"));

Result: Cleaner, reusable, and readable code — a hallmark of a good automation framework.

Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)

Method Overriding occurs when a child class provides a new implementation for a method that is already defined in its parent class. It is known as runtime polymorphism because the method to be executed is determined at runtime, depending on the object type.

Key Points:

• Involves two classes (Parent and Child) connected via inheritance.
• The method name, return type, and parameters must be the same.
• Used to customize or extend behavior of parent methods.
• Achieved through inheritance and the @Override annotation.

Real-Time Example in Test Automation:

Imagine you have a BaseTest class that handles common setup and teardown logic for all tests. However, different test suites (like Web, Mobile, or API) might need to override setup steps as per their environment.

// Parent class - Common setup for all tests
public class BaseTest {
    public void setUp() {
        System.out.println("Launching browser: Chrome");
        // Code to initialize WebDriver and open application
    }
}

Now, suppose you have a subclass for mobile testing where the setup needs to use Appium instead of Selenium:

// Child class - Specific setup for mobile testing
public class MobileTest extends BaseTest {

    @Override
    public void setUp() {
        System.out.println("Launching mobile app using Appium");
        // Code to initialize Appium driver
    }
}

Example in Test Script:

BaseTest test1 = new BaseTest();
test1.setUp();  // Output: Launching browser: Chrome
BaseTest test2 = new MobileTest();
test2.setUp();  // Output: Launching mobile app using Appium

Result: The overridden method in the child class (MobileTest) executes instead of the parent’s method (BaseTest), even though the reference type is of the parent class. This is runtime polymorphism in action — the JVM decides at runtime which version to execute.

Method Overloading vs Method Overriding — Key Differences


Real-Time Application in Selenium Frameworks


Conclusion

• Method Overloading helps us use the same method name for different input variations, leading to cleaner, reusable, and flexible code.
• Method Overriding allows customizing or extending the behavior of parent class methods, essential in creating scalable, maintainable automation frameworks.

In test automation, overloading improves utility flexibility (like login, click, wait functions), while overriding empowers framework extensibility (like environment setup, driver management, or reporting).

Both concepts together make your automation framework robust, object-oriented, and adaptable to change.

